%description:

RFC 1122 -- a missing table entry alone produces no Destination Unreachable report.

Checks RFC1122-ANOERR-1 (must not) from doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc1122/catalog.md.
The English procedure is in
doc/project/evidence/protocol/arp/checks/no-error-report.md#no-destination-unreachable; the
program steps below map onto its expected observations. Mapping of the absent neighbour
mockup: host A = hostA, host B = hostB, and 10.0.0.99 is the address that no station owns.

The address is on the link, so it is routable and no host can call it unreachable for a
routing reason. No datagram reaches any station, so no port can be closed either. The
missing table entry is the only reason for a report that the scenario leaves, and that
reason is the forbidden one.

The absence watch looks for any ICMP message and not for the Destination Unreachable type
alone. Nothing in this scenario gives a host a reason to send any ICMP message, so the wider
watch costs nothing and catches a host that reports the failure as another type.

Deviation: expected observation 2, that no reply for the address ever crosses the link, is
not a program step, because the absence watch of expected observation 3 consumes the rest of
the run. It is asserted instead on the line that the program of host B prints at the end of
the run: host B received nothing, so no resolution succeeded.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "ArpMutations.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("rfc1122_no_destination_unreachable")
        // Expected observation 1: the resolution really was attempted.
        .once(on("hostA.eth[0].mac").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .packet("arp.opcode == 1")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) {
                      return ipv4AddressField(c.event.packet, "arp.destIpAddress") == "10.0.0.99";
                  })
                  .describe("an ARP request from host A for 10.0.0.99")
                  .within(0.5))
        // Expected observation 3: no ICMP message anywhere at host A, for the rest of the
        // run (RFC1122-ANOERR-1).
        .never(on("hostA")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) {
                      return matchesExpression(c.event.packet, "icmpv4.type >= 0");
                  })
                  .describe("any ICMP message at host A")
                  .within(9.4));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc1122_no_destination_unreachable compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;

// The absent neighbour mockup: two hosts on one Ethernet link, and a third address on the
// link that no station owns.
network ArpPairNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        hostA: StandardHost { @display("p=300,100"); }
        hostB: StandardHost { @display("p=500,100"); }
    connections:
        hostA.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> hostB.ethg++; // link 1
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = ArpPairNet
sim-time-limit = 10s
seed-set = 0

# Fixed addresses, so that the address nobody owns is a known one.
*.configurator.config = xml("<config><interface hosts='hostA' address='10.0.0.1' netmask='255.255.255.0'/><interface hosts='hostB' address='10.0.0.2' netmask='255.255.255.0'/></config>")

# A datagram every 0.5 s to 10.0.0.99, which no station owns.
*.hostA.numApps = 1
*.hostA.app[0].typename = "UdpBasicApp"
*.hostA.app[0].destAddresses = "10.0.0.99"
*.hostA.app[0].destPort = 5000
*.hostA.app[0].localPort = 4000
*.hostA.app[0].messageLength = 100B
*.hostA.app[0].startTime = 0.1s
*.hostA.app[0].sendInterval = 0.5s
*.hostB.numApps = 1
*.hostB.app[0].typename = "UdpSink"
*.hostB.app[0].localPort = 5000

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc1122_no_destination_unreachable: PASS

%contains: stdout
hostB.app[0]: received 0 packets
